<h2>Ways to lower your lighting cost</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Replace halogen with LED.</strong> The single largest reduction for older systems.</li>
<li><strong>Trim the schedule.</strong> Few homes need every accent light on until dawn.</li>
<li><strong>Zone the system.</strong> Give entries, paths, architecture and entertaining areas their own schedules.</li>
<li><strong>Dim late at night.</strong> Lower output on decorative fixtures after midnight.</li>
<li><strong>Remove redundant fixtures.</strong> One well-aimed wide beam can replace two poorly placed lights.</li>
<li><strong>Clean and re-aim.</strong> A dirty or misaimed fixture looks weak, which tempts people to install a brighter lamp. Cleaning it costs nothing.</li>
<li><strong>Fix wiring and connections.</strong> Corroded splices and voltage drop make fixtures dim and uneven.</li>
<li><strong>Revisit after the landscape grows.</strong> Gulf Coast plants grow fast, and buried fixtures waste their output.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Signs an older system is wasting energy</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixtures that are too hot to touch</li>
<li>Frequent lamp failures</li>
<li>Lights running in daylight</li>
<li>Bright fixtures near the transformer and dim ones at the far end of a run</li>
<li>Corroded connections or exposed wire</li>
<li>A humming or overheating transformer</li>
<li>No timer, or one that resets after every outage</li>
<li>Halogen lamps mixed with newer LEDs</li>
</ul>
<p>Installing higher-wattage lamps is rarely the right fix for dim lights. The wiring layout, connections, cable size or transformer settings usually need attention instead, which is the kind of work our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">landscape lighting repair</a> visits handle.</p>

<h2>Why design matters more than fixture count</h2>
<p>Outdoor lighting is not a contest to fit the most fixtures into a yard. Too many lights create glare, flatten the texture of brick and stone, and wash out plantings. A single carefully aimed uplight can reveal a column, a wide beam can carry a live oak canopy, and a shielded path light can guide feet without shining into anyone's eyes.</p>
<blockquote class="pull">The goal is not maximum brightness. It is useful, comfortable light in the right places.</blockquote>
<p>When placement, beam spread and brightness are right, fewer fixtures often look better, and that lowers material cost, maintenance and electricity at the same time. If you are planning a new system, the <a href="/tools/fixture-estimator/">fixture estimator</a> gives a starting count, and a <a href="/quote/">design consultation</a> refines it.</p>

